@scene(20)
// Defines a simple ship hull shape using a profile of varying elliptical cross-sections.
// The hull is aligned along the x-axis and centered at the origin.
// hull_length: The total length of the hull (bow to stern) along the x-axis.
// width: The maximum width of the hull (beam) along the y-axis.
// height: The maximum height of the hull (from keel to deck) along the z-axis.
ship_hull(hull_length, width, height) {
	half_len = hull_length / 2.0;
	half_width = width / 2.0;
	half_height = height / 2.0;

	// Calculate the hull's profile scale factor using an ellipse shape.
	// This makes the hull profile elliptical from a top-down or side view.
	// The value is 1.0 at the center (x=0) and 0.0 at the ends (x=+/-half_len).
	profile_sq = 1.0 - (p.x * p.x) / (half_len * half_len);
	profile = sqrt(max(0.0, profile_sq));

	// Determine the radii of the elliptical cross-section at the current point's x-coordinate.
	ry = half_width * profile;
	rz = half_height * profile;

	// A small value to prevent division by zero when at the bow or stern where radii are zero.
	epsilon = 0.0001;

	// Calculate the distance from the point to the surface of an infinite elliptical cylinder
	// with the radii calculated above. This creates the curved hull surface.
	dist_surface = length([p.y / (ry + epsilon), p.z / (rz + epsilon)]) - 1.0;

	// Calculate the distance from the point to the capping planes at the bow and stern.
	// This bounds the hull to its specified length.
	dist_caps = abs(p.x) - half_len;

	// The final shape is the intersection of the infinite hull surface and the capping volume.
	// The max() function combines the two distance fields to produce their intersection.
	return max(dist_surface, dist_caps);
}

// Create a ship hull with a length of 8.0, width 2.0, and height 1.5,
// fitting within the requested scene dimensions.
s = ship_hull(8.0, 2.0, 1.5);

// Return the final signed distance function for the ship.
return s;
